Introduction:

This document is divided into four parts, the first is a basic installation
introduction and overview.  The second is a trouble shooting section,
if you have problems setting up TERM read this section.  The third
is a more detailed function description.  And the fourth is a "changes"
section, once you have TERM installed and working you will want to
read this section to learn about what changes were made in a new version
you have just obtained.  You might also have to read the Changes section
(at least the most recent parts) if you have just picked up a copy
of TERM since I might not have updated the other sections...


Installation and operation of TERM has been greatly simplified, in
fact all you need to do is to drag the two icons "TERM" and
"Demo_Script" to where you want TERM to live.  Double clicking the
TERM icon will then dial you into VBBS and log you on.

(well almost...)

Before you double click the TERM icon there are a few things you will
need to do.  First use the WorkBench's Info menu item to edit the
tooltypes in the TERM icon.  You should change the NAME= and PASSWORD=
tooltypes to specify your name and your password.  If you have a 1200
baud modem you should change the BAUD= tooltype to read BAUD=1200.
You should also consider setting up the following tooltypes:

      MESSROOT=dirname
      EMAILROOT=dirname
      DOWNDIR=dirname
      TEMPDIR=dirname
      EDITOR=programname
      KEEPFILE=filename

These will greatly improve life with TERM.  Here is a quick run down
on these:  (the "dirname" means these tooltype expect to be assigned
to a full AmigaDOS directory name, you should include the drive in this
too, like:  TEMPDIR=dh1:t)

   MESSROOT=  This is used to specify a directory where you want the
              "Sigs" tool to download the messages bases to.  This in
              an "off-line" reading/replying/posting system (to minimize
              connect time) so you will need to make this directory
              on a disk with some space.  The directory must exist
              before you start TERM (and hit the Sigs button) and you
              should NOT store anything else in this directory.

   EMAILROOT= This is used specify a directory where you want any EMail
              that is sent to you to be saved and where the User List
              is also to be saved between TERM sessions (so you don't
              have to keep downloading it).  Again this directory should
              exist before you start using EMail.

   DOWNDIR=   This is used to specify a directory where you want any files
              you download to be put.  Note that you cannot currently
              change this directory while you are running TERM except
              by setting it to a logical name like "DOWNDIR=fred:" and
              then either inserting one of several disks called "Fred"
              or by using the CLI command Assign to change the location
              of the Fred: assign. (These should work but I have not
              tried -- the limitation will be removed when I get the time)

   TEMPDIR=   This is where TERM will store a number of different
              temporary files it creates from time to time.  You might
              assign this to "t:" or perhaps "ram:".

   EDITOR=    TERM allows you to use the editor of your choice to compose
              email, file descriptions, and messages.  The default is
              "ED" and you will probably want to change that real fast!
              The limitation is the editor MUST be in your C: directory
              (at least under 1.3).

   KEEPFILE=  When you are reading messages in the "Feedback" tool you
              can save ones of interest by just hitting a button, these
              messages are saved in the file specified by this tooltype.
              Please note that you should include the full path to the
              file, like:  KEEPFILE=dh1:vterm/stuff/junk/fred/goodies
              where "goodies" is the file which will contain the saved
              messages.

Lastly, if you do not have ARP installed on your system you will need
to execute the following command from the CLI:

     copy AMUC_VOL_52A:showcase/arp.library LIBS:

For most users that is all you have to do to install the TERM program
for VBBS, all you have to do now is to double click the icon.

If you use an internal modem or a modem attached to an expansion board
with extra serial ports you will probably want to change the UNIT=
and DEVICE= tooltypes as well.

If you have any problems logging into VBBS read the following section
first and then give me a call at 282-7990 or leave a message for me
on the AMUC Express.


COMMON PROBLEMS!!!!!

The following are a few tips for first time users of the VBBS-TERM
package.  All of these gotchas are fairly common, so if something does
not work make certain you look at these first!

   0)  TERM refuses to start and immediatly returns to the CLI prompt.

       You need to check your LIBS: to see if you have ARP.LIBRARY
       installed, also check to see that you are not already using the
       serial port.  Could also be that you are low on memory.

   1)  The initial terminal window goes away then reappears instead
       of the control window.

       Your modem cable probably is not passing the CARRIER DETECT line.

   2)  When I type stuff into the initial terminal window all I get
       is garbage.

       You probably have the BAUD rate set incorrectly. Note that TERM
       gets its baud rate from PREFERENCES, unless you start it with a
       line like:

           TERM -dserial.device -u0 -b1200

       or you might have the baud rate incorrectly specified in the
       TERM icon's tool type array, check the BAUD= entry.

   3)  I can read messages but cannot post new ones or reply to old ones.

       This is typically caused by your not editing the "editor" line
       in the TERM icon's tool type array.  This may also be caused by
       not running TERM with enough stack space.  Try the CLI command:

             STACK 10000

       just before running TERM.

   4)  How do I log in under my own name?

       Simple!  Just edit the TOOLTYPES in the TERM icon.  There is an
       entry called "NAME=" for your name and "PASSWORD=" for your
       password.

   5)  When I call the BBS I get a bad connection or I cannot login at
       2400 baud or the BBS hangs up on me.

       Solution, call again and if it happens again wait 1 minute,
       the BBS does a complete reset of its serial port and modem
       a short while after it sees the phone ring if no one has
       been able to log in.  Some modems occasionally screwup their
       baud rate and/or parity settings and until they reset the BBS
       cannot understand what they are saying.

   6)  The Talk window opens and then immediatly closes.

       You need to have the translator.library in LIBS: and the
       narrator.device in DEVS:  Often people strip these from their
       system to save space on the boot disk.  It is safe to run TERM
       without these present, but you will not be able to page the
       Sysop.

   7)  TERM wants to write to my boot disk.

       TERM needs to create some temporary files from time to time.
       It puts these files in the directory "T:".  By default this
       directory will be the :T directory on your boot disk.  You need
       to add a command like:

            ASSIGN T: RAM:

       to your startup sequence.  This tells your Amiga to put temp files
       into the ram disk.  Alternatively, you can edit the TOOLTYPES in
       the TERM icon and add an entry called "TEMPDIR=", pointing to the
       directory you wish to use.

   8)  When I exit term with the Quit menu item the modem still stays on
       line, I want it to hang up the connection.

       The problem here is that your modem has been set to ignore the
       DTR line, when TERM exits it closes the serial port which
       should drop the DTR line.  This should cause the modem to drop
       the line.  However, modems can be set to ignore this line,
       and/or your serial cable may not pass this line.  If your modem
       is ignoring the line you may wish to send it an AT&D3 command
       before starting up TERM.  You can make this setting permanant
       by issuing an AT&W0 and AT&W1 command.  Note: some people do not
       like to use the AT&D3 setting as they like to hop from one
       terminal package to another without the modem hanging up the
       connection.

   9)  I get massive quantities of line noise resulting in very slow
       file transfers.

       This can result from having the wrong handshaking between your
       modem and serial port.  There are two modes available: either
       "-hn" (no handshaking) "-h7" (RTS/CTS handshake, 7 wire mode).
       It should not matter which you choose, except that if you are
       running a newer modem where the serial rate can be higher than
       the modem's phone line rate then you MUST use the 7 wire mode.

       NOTE: since TERM/VBBS uses a full 8-bit binary connection you
       cannot select the XON/XOFF handshaking.  If you do, TERM will
       default to no handshaking.

   10) I can read messages but I cannot post new ones.

       This could be because you are using an editor that detaches
       from the CLI.  The editors: AZ and CygnusEd are known to do
       this.  Try a different editor.  Actually TERM can now handle
       these editors too, just wait until you are finshed editing your
       message and have saved the file BEFORE clicking on the TERM
       requester that asks you if you want to post the message.

   11) I am using the Applied Engineering internal modem board for
       the 2000,  and cannot get anything to happen.

       Make certain you send the modem the command "ATC1".

   12) fixed.

   13) I am using an internal modem (either the SupraModem or the
       Applied Engineering one) and strange things are happening.

       Earlier versions of the Applied Engineering modem would not
       handle multiple character reads correctly, new versions of
       the modem's ROM software fix the problem.

       I can recompile the TERM program so that it does not do multiple
       character IO, this will patch the problem until you get the
       board fixed.  Contact me about the single character IO version.

   14) Various things in TERM don't seem to want to work.

       For some reason running TERM from an IconX shell script when
       the Shell-Seg is resident prevents term downloads from working.

       Also DON'T run one of those "cancel requesters" programs while
       you are using term, the result will be that you cannot download
       files or post new messages (or replys) while the requester
       canceler is in the system.  Actually, it might be safe to do this
       now, as I have replaced most of the AutoRequest() calls with my
       own routine.

   15) When I run "The Enforcer" (a developer's MMU utility) and TERM,
       the enforcer reports violations when I use the Talk function,
       the Message reader and the file downloader.

       These violations are probably due to the AutoRequest() function
       and the Translate() function.  You should find these disappear
       when TERM is run under 2.0 (it appears that you can use the 2.0
       translate.library in a system running 1.3, so if you have 2.0
       try that).

   16) If you find that the message reader locks up or crashes after
       posting or replying to a message -- and you are running TERM
       from the WorkBench -- remove the line in your startup-sequence
       which says "resident Shell-Seg" or else run TERM from the CLI.

   17) If you are using an MNP capable modem and cannot use TERM once
       the connection process is complete and the control window says
       you are `Logged In', then take a look at your modem settings
       you should set AT\G0.  (This was the Al K. problem).

   18) My script file prints garbage or TERM crashes on exit.

       Are you running DMouse or some other form of windowing enhancer?
       Try running TERM without it enabled and see if the problem
       goes away.  Whatever the outcome, report it to me!

   19) My Packard Bell modem does not seem to dial when I use a script file.

       On some modems it seems that the "cr" command in the script file
       (which sends a carriage return line feed pair) confuses the modem.
       The solution to this is to replace all "cr" commands with "char 13"
       commands (which will just send a carriage return).

How to use TERM:

                        FROM THE WORKBENCH:

Just check the tooltypes array (in the TERM icon) to see that the
entries are correct for your system.  Make certain that the "demo_script"
is in the same drawer as the TERM program -- or else correct the
SCRIPT= tool type) and then double click the icon.  TERM will dial
up VBBS and log you in.  For an explaination of the various tool types
fields see the next section.

You may have to edit the UNIT= and DEVICE= tooltypes if you are using
an internal modem card or a serial port on an expansion board.  Also,
you may need to edit the BAUD= tooltype to suit your modem.

If you are running Commodore's shell (or using a relatively unmodified
1.3 startup-sequence) you should see problem #16 in the above list.

                           FROM THE CLI:

NOTE: CLI users will need to keep the TERM.info (icon) file around
because this is used to specify the various parameters that TERM
needs to run.  Because CLI users do not like their directories
cluttered with .info files, when TERM is started from the CLI it will
first look in the current directory for the TERM.info file (if you
rename TERM to FarOutTerminal you will need to rename the
icon to FarOutTerminal.info as well), if it does not find it there it
will then look in the S: directory and if it fails to find it there
it will look in the C: directory.

First you need to establish a phone connection, do this by starting
the VBBS terminal program called "TERM".  The arguments TERM accepts
are the serial.device name, the unit number, the baud rate, the
handshaking type and the name of a script file to run.  These
arguments are optional. If you have a second serial board, you can use
it by suppling the appropriate name of the serial device and the unit
number.  If you just supply the serial device name and not the unit
number it will open unit zero.  Finally, if you specify an alternate
serial device and unit but no baud rate the system will use whatever
baud rate is specified in PREFERENCES.

   TERM [scriptfile] -dserial.device -uunitno -bbaudrate -hhandshake

for example to use the ASDG serial card, unit number 1, at 4800 baud
with no handshaking you would use

   TERM demo_script -b4800 -u1 -dsiosbx.device -hn

To run TERM with a MNP modem connected on the serial port at 9600 baud
either:

   TERM demo_script -b9600 -h7

or

   TERM demo_script -b9600 -h7 -dserial.device -u0

will do the trick.

The script file is the name of a TERM script file to run to get yourself
dialed in.

NOTE: all the command line parameters are applied after TERM reads
the configuration information from the icon, so if you wish to override
the icon settings without changing the icon you may do so.

A window will appear on the workbench which you can use to talk
directly to the serial port.  You would probably send a command to
your modem next to get it to dial my number:

    atdp 284-2048

will probably suffice.  My system, running the VBBS bbs module, will
answer the phone and a connection will be established.  The initial
terminal window will display the login welcome message and then the
error correcting multiplexing protocol will be started.  Once this has
been done a small window will appear (initially called "Control
window") and when the connection is complete the name will be changed
to "Logged in".

Now the protocol has started you can select one of five things: Chat
Send File, Messages, Talk or End Protocol.  You select Chat to start a new
chat window, Send File to send some data to the other computer's RAM:
disk, Messages to read the new messages since you last read
them (and to post new messages), Talk to type in messages that
will be SPOKEN at the other end and End Protocol to quit.  Note you
cannot quit while there are any open channels (chat windows or
send/receive file windows).

This system also supports the carrier detect line if your modem and
cable supports it.  When the carrier is lost the system will shut down
and return to the initial terminal window.  If a send file requester
is on the screen you will have to click on it's close gadget to close
it down.  Note that at the moment your modem cable MUST support this
line, otherwise TERM will never enter the error correcting protocol
mode.

Exiting from TERM.  There are two ways to quit from TERM.  The first
is to close down any open communications windows and then select
the End Protocol menu item.  This method is preferred since it waits
for any buffered messages to be sent before allowing the exit to take
place.  You can also quit by just causing the phone connection to be
broken (switching the modem off usually does this), this will
automatically cause a complete shutdown.

Support files...........

The TERM program currently needs ARP.LIBRARY in libs:, as well it
needs its icon file (TERM.info) from which it will get default
parameters. The icon contains various tooltypes which allow you to set
your user ID (USER=) and password (PASSWORD=)  you should edit these
to select an account name for yourself and a password.

The EDITOR= tooltype specifies the path to your favourite editor
(the default is "ed" which is nobody's favourite). If you do not
specify the directory the editor is in (and you only supply the name
of the editor) then the editor will be loaded from the C: directory.
NOTE:  THIS MEANS THE PATH WILL NOT BE SEARCHED.  THE EDITOR WILL ONLY
BE LOADED IF YOU HAVE IT IN YOUR C: DIRECTORY.  Do I make myself
clear?

The CONFHISTORY= tooltype enters a number which specifies the
number of lines that are to be kept in the conference window's
scrollable history buffer.  A value such as 30-50 is appropriate.

The HAILSTRING= and TAILSTRING= tooltypes contain text or control
characters you want sent with each paragraph you type in the
conference window to identify your messages.  The first is the text
you want put at the start of your message and the second is the text
you want put at the end of your message.

There are a number of other tooltypes which are explained later on,
although their names should make them pretty obvious.

A sample icon is included.  Note that when the system sees an unknown name
it automatically sets up a new account and records that name and
password on the BBS hard disk for future reference.

------------------------ COMMAND LINE PARAMETERS ----------------------

The following command line parameters are supported by the TERM program:

   -b   Sets the baud rate
        -b9600 (9600 baud)
        -b1200 (1200 baud)

   -d   Selects the serial device to be used
        -dserial.device (the default Amiga serial port)
        -dsiosbx.device (the ASDG dual serial card)

   -h   Selects the serial port handshaking mode to be used
        -hn  (no handshaking, the default)
        -h7  (no hand shake using the 7-wire (CTS/RTS) protocol.

   -u   Selects the unit number of the serial device to be used
        -u0  (the default (Amiga serial) or the lower connector on the ASDG
              card)
        -u1  (the second connector (upper) on the ASDG card)

NOTE: command line parameters will override the defaults that were set
up by tooltypes in the icon.  So if you want to change something temporarily
you can just specify the command line parameter for it.

----------------------- Using the Message Section ----------------------

VBBS and TERM 0.5 and higher support message posting and reading operations.
To access the message base select the Messages menu item once the connection
has been established.  Doing this will open up the message reader window
and cause the VBBS to send all the new messages to your machine.  The
first message will be displayed and then the output will pause waiting
for you to finish reading and select one of the options by pressing
the appropriate key:

    RETURN  -- preceed to the next message
    R       -- fire up the editor so you can write a reply to
               this message
    P       -- post a new message on a different topic.
    K       -- appends a copy of the current message to your keepfile.
    SPACE   -- messages that are longer than a screen will automatically
               pause when the screen has been filled, you can then hit
               the space bar to get to the next screen full.

In order for you to post new messages and to reply to existing messages
you need to have a text editor in your C: directory or else you must
specify the full path to the editor with the "EDITOR=" tooltype (see above).
This editor can be any editor you can run from the CLI with a command
like:

    myeditor  filename

So when you press either the "P" or "R" keys TERM will call up your
editor to allow you to compose your message.

When you are editing a reply all the text in your editor will be sent to
the BBS as the reply to the messsage; however, when you are posting
a new message ("P") the FIRST LINE of your message will be placed
in the SUBJECT field of the message header.


----------------------- USING THE TALK WINDOW ------------------------

VBBS 0.6 now supports a "Talk" window which is similar to the Chat window
except what you type will be SPOKEN at both ends.  This is useful for
paging the Sysop and so that the Sysop can annoy you without filling
your screen...  So when you log on and want to find out if the Sysop
is nearby type something into the talk window and hit return.  Note the
talk window does not send anything to the other machine UNTIL you hit
return.

Stephen Vermeulen 25-Feb-1990


------------------- USING GET FILES, THE FILE LIBRARY ----------------

VBBS 0.8 now supports a file library which you can browse through and
download files from.  Currently, the user interface for this feature
is in the prototype phase, it is fully functional but quite crude.  I
would appreciate feedback on this issue for a later release.  With that
proviso out of the way, you access the file library on the BBS by using
the "Get Files" menu item from the main control window menu.  When this
is done a new window will popup.  Initially this contains "Parent"
and two lines with "^^^   vvv" on them.  The parent string is a gadget
that, when clicked on, will move you to the parent of the current
directory.  The "^^^" scrolls the current file or directory list up
by 5 items when clicked on.  The "vvv" scrolls the current file or directory list
down by 5 items when clicked on.  After a short pause the upper section
of the window will show several directory names, these are the directories
you have access to on the BBS machine's hard drive.  There should be at
least one directory visable here, and there may be many.  The names
presented here are not really of interest to you but are important to the
Sysop.  Clicking on one of these directory names will move you into that
directory and will show you the directories and files within the
directory.  Everytime you select a new directory your terminal program
will ask the BBS for a list of files within that directory and the BBS
will send it that list.  If you exit a directory and then re-enter it
later you will find that the file list reappears immediatly since the
TERMinal program stores the file lists it receives locally.  When you
want to download a file just click on the filename in the lower half
of the window.  Doing this will cause a requester to appear showing
the name of the file and its size that you have selected for download.
If you answer Yes to this requester then the download will commence and
you can go on to do other things on the BBS, in fact you could even
go back to the files window and request to download something else,
note if you do this the download window for the second request may not
appear for a while since the files are sent in 5K byte bursts.

In version 14 the file requester was enhanced to be resizable and to
display the file sizes (in bytes) to the right of the file names.  It should
also be possible to make this requester very tall on an interlaced
screen.

------------------------- USING THE SCRIPT FILE ------------------------

TERM now supports a script file which can be used to allow for automatic
redial until you are logged in.  The commands for the script file are:

   send STRING
   delay N
   goto LABEL
   wait STRING
   on STRING LABEL
   exit
   cr
   char N
   print STRING
   ;this is a sample comment

Comments in the script file are initiated with the ";" character.
All following characters until the end of the line are considered
to be part of the comment.  Blank lines are allowed in the script
if you wish.

A script may be automatically run when TERM is started either from
the CLI or from an icon.  In the icon use the "SCRIPT=" tooltype
to specify the name of the script file you want TERM to run.

The "send" command sends the following string of text to the serial port.
STRING may be a single word such as:

   Xyzzy

or it may be a word or words enclosed in double quotes ("), such as:

  "this is a sample STRING"

The "delay" command delays for N tenths of a second (N is a number).

The "goto" command causes the script to jump to the indicated label in
the script (either fowards or backwards) and execution will continue
following the label.  You may have as many labels as you like, labels
may be any combinations of ascii characters which do not make up one
of the above reserved command words.

The "wait" command causes execution of the script to pause until the
indicated STRING (as defined above) is received on the serial port or
another string that triggers an exception is received.

The "on" command is used to add a new exception string to the list of
strings which, when received on the serial port, will cause an immediate
jump to the specified label.  You may have as many exception strings
active as you wish.  Specifing the same string a second time will erase
the first specification allowing you to change the branch label.

The "exit" command is used to terminate the script file and put the
TERM program into it's listen for auto-connect sequence mode.

The "cr" command is used to send a cariage return (well line feed  and
carriage return pair actually) to the serial port.

The "char" command will send a single ascii character (identified by
its ASCII code number) to the serial port.

The "print" command will cause the following string to be printed on
the display window, but nothing is sent to the serial port.  This is
useful for printing status messages telling you what the script is
currently doing.

Here is a brief autodial sequence...

-----------------------------------------------
init

   on CONNECT startproto
   on BUSY redial
   on NOCARRIER redial

redial

  send ATZ
  cr
  wait OK
  send ATDP2842048
  cr
  delay 100
  goto redial

startproto

  exit
-----------------------------------------------

----------------------------   CHANGES  --------------------------------

65   Fixed a bug in Sigs that could cause memory to get trashed
     (encapsulated the function call in asm register save/restore
     like the rest...)

64   Added new-look screen feature for 2.0 users -- now TERM will look
     much nicer if you open it on a custom screen under 2.0.  Also
     added a requester to the EMail that will prompt you for a new
     directory for EMAILROOT if it can't find the directory you specified.

63   Added the "Send Fido" button to EMail.  Fixed a bug in the window
     opening function to prevent snapshots that are off the current
     screen (ie. were made on a bigger screen) from taking effect.
     Added ability to receive and reply to FIDO net mail.  Added send
     ability to FIDO, just hit the Send Fido button and fill in the
     string requesters that pop up.  If you have messed up the MESSROOT
     definition TERM will prompt you for a valid one.  Finally, at long
     last, added a DownDir button to allow you to change the download
     directory while term is running.

62   Fixed the bug in Get Files that could strike you dead (or result in
     truncated or scrambled lists of files) if you clicked on Parent
     or in the directory area too quickly.

61   The pop up "yes/no" requesters will now try to appear underneath
     your mouse.  This is especially useful for those lucky A3000
     owners who run TERM on WorkBench screens that are several screens
     large...  You should no-longer be able to confuse EMail by repeatedly
     requesting a user list.

60   Fixed a bug in the emailing of files where the file name could get
     corrupted.  Fixed a bug in the text list handling routines that
     prevented you from clicking on the last item of a list.

59   Email now sorts the user name list.  Fixed a bug that could cause
     TERM to crash when the "inactive do something" requester came up
     and you were using a MNP/V42 connection.  Email now keeps old
     messages in your EMAILROOT/VBBS_Host directory (until you delete
     them).

     DO NOT set up your MESSROOT and EMAILROOT directories to use the
     same directory!  READ cannot read EMail.

     If you set MESSROOT=ram: you can have problems, don't do this, it
     defeats the basic design of the Sigs system.

58   Fixed a small bug in EMail, if you had just cleaned/created your
     local email directory and there was email waiting for you on the
     BBS machine you would have problems replying to it.  Fixed now.
     Was an exclusive directory lock from CreateDir("VBBS_Host").

57   Now the "Beep" button in the monitor will no longer beep if you
     have turned off the "Bell".  Added EMail with both file and message
     transfer capability between users.  You can test this by EMailing
     yourself.

     Note: yet another tooltype has been added to the TERM icon, this
     is the EMAILROOT= setting which you should set to a directory
     where you want your email messages to be placed upon receipt.
     Note that email files get sent to your download directory (since
     they are received by the file downloader).  When you snapshot the
     email window(s) some additional tooltypes will be created as well.

     Apparently there may be a bug in my scrolling list routines, for this
     reason you are cautioned against manipulating list windows while the
     bbs is sending new data to be displayed in the list window.

56   Enhanced the Monitor, VBBS Message and Get Files windows to use
     a single list display routine.  This provides you with enhanced
     functionality while reducing the size of TERM (before adding
     some more features).  The changes required the addition of several
     new tool types:

      GETFILE_BTNS    The buttons window for Get Files
      GETFILE_FILES   The file name requester window for Get Files
      GETFILE_DIRS    The directory name requester window for Get Files
      MON_BTNS        The buttons window for the Monitor tool
      MON_LIST        The event list window for the Monitor tool

     and the removal of the GETFILEWINDOW and MONWINDOW tooltypes.
     Also allowed for snapshotting of the Describe/Confirm gadgets in the
     Getfiles tool and the Flash/Bell gadgets in the Monitor tool.  The
     tooltypes for these are:

      GETFILE_DESC    Enables long descriptions to be sent to you
      GETFILE_CFRM    Enables the "do you want to..." requester
      MON_BELL        Enables the bell on your terminal
      MON_FLASH       Enables the screen flashing

     Added an interlock to the Sigs button so that you can only start
     one of them at a time.

55   Revised the tooltypes processing so that now you can enter
     tooltype names (the part to the left of the "=" sign) in
     either upper or lower case (or any mixture of the two).
     Added a new version of the protocol, which is automatically
     activated by the BBS if you make an error-corrected connection
     (such as an HST, MNP, or V42 type protocol).  With this method
     there is virtually no overhead on the data transfer between
     VBBS and TERM, as well the processing requirements are off-loaded
     to the modem to some extent.

54   Added the Monitor client.  This allows you to see who else is
     on line and what they are doing.  As well it contains a "Beep"
     button you can use to page all those who are on line and for those
     who might get annoyed at your beeping there are two additional
     buttons to enable/disable the audio beeper (Bell) and the
     screen flasher (Flash).  By default both these are enabled
     (button pressed in).  Note that whenever you hit beep there will be
     a beep at your end, and if you have "Bell" enabled you will get
     a second beep a moment later.  Also, note that the only time
     someone can be paged is if he has the monitor window opened.

53   Changed the multiplexer/protocol handshaking to use semaphores
     instead of Forbid/Permit.  Added an abort script function, hit
     the ESC key to stop a script.  Fixed a bug in script files that
     could result in trashed memory -- this would also result in the
     garbage character printed after the string in the print and cr
     commands.  Reversed the date sort, so most recent dates appear
     on top.  Added some diagnostic requesters to script files.
     Fixed a Remove() function call in scripts.

52   Adjusted the priority on the Sigs function, it is now the same
     as the file send/receive functions so it should no longer lock
     out file transfers.

51   Found a nasty little bug in the low level protocol.  Every time
     a NAK was received from the remote end there was a possibility
     of corrupting some memory.  This is probably why Craig Lever's
     Sister caused so much havoc.

50   This version fixes up the times with Messages a bit more.  Should
     be the last fix needed.  Note that you will be effectively deleting
     all the messages you have downloaded, so it would be best if you
     delete the VBBS_host directory and all its contents (including
     subdirectories) so that we get a clean start.  Now TERM does some
     checking on the validity of your MESSROOT directory.

49   Changed the new message base gadget to "Sigs" and the old message
     base gadget to "Feedback".  The gadgets in the Message Filter window
     now do something -- they just tell you they are not implemented yet.
     Fixed the problem with snapshotting that could cause the rightmost
     main Messages gadget to be hidden.

     Now the BBS supports "optional sigs", the sysop configures the
     sigs you will receive by default until you create a MySigs file.
     I have set this up so you only see two sigs initially.  This was
     done because the USENET connection is bringing a LOT of stuff
     online and you should select just the ones you want.

     Note that because TERM locks the VBBS_host directory while it
     is using it you may not be able to look into that directory with
     the READ utility or with AmigaDOS!

48   Term now touches all the vbbs.keyfiles in the parent directories
     of a directory where it has just stored a new message, this allows
     Read to always descend to find new files.  SIG filtration has been
     added to TERM.  Right now the interface is a bit crude, but it'll
     do the job.  Whenever you connect to a VBBS system and select the
     Messages gadget, the server will send your system a full list of the
     sigs that are available, your system will write this into the
     current BBS's directory (VBBS_Host) in the file "AllSigs".
     Then your system will request from the BBS an update of only
     those sigs that are listed in the "MySigs" file.  If the MySigs
     file does not exist you will get an update on all the Sigs.  This
     has been added so that when my usenet newsfeed is feeding directly
     into the VBBS Message base you can shut off the news groups you
     don't want.

47   The vbbs.lastread file has been moved again, it should now appear
     within the VBBS_Host directory (the name of my BBS), this allows
     TERM to track when you last logged onto each different VBBS system
     you called.  Also the lastread file is set by the time at the VBBS
     end, so you don't have to have an accurate clock anymore.

     For those of you who like to stay in sync, or who don't have
     battery backed up clocks, the new tooltype SETCLOCK= when
     set to "yes" will allow TERM to change your machine's system time
     to match that of VBBS.  The default setting is "no".

46   Moved the s:vbbs.lastread file into the MESSROOT directory.  Fixed
     some forgotton file locks.  Now writes the correct dates to the
     vbbs.keyfile files so that scans work.  Note you should delete your
     complete message directory tree and get a fresh copy.

45   Added the sort by "Name" and "Date" gadgets to the Get Files window.
     The "Messages" button now does something...  All the little buttons
     within the message filter window still do nothing; however, upon
     selecting the message filter tool you will start to build a copy
     of the message tree that exists on the BBS on your machine.
     To make this work, you must add the MESSROOT= tooltype to your
     TERM icon.  This should be set to a convenient directory where
     you want your copy of the message tree build.  If it is now specified
     the message tree will be built in RAM:.  You will probably want this
     on a physical disk so that you can refer to messages when not
     logged into VBBS.

     Once the MESSROOT= tooltype is set, just call VBBS, select the
     "Messages" gadget and wait until the new status display tells you
     it is done.  After you close the message filter window you will
     be able to view the messages.  Try a "DIR dirname OPT A" on the
     name of the root directory you specified in MESSROOT=, you should
     see a number of files and subdirectories.

     Now, your machine will keep track of the date you last read messages,
     TERM stores it in a file called S:VBBS.LASTREAD, its only a 4 byte
     long file containing the last time (in seconds) you requested a
     message update.  Whenever you select the "Messages" gadget in
     TERM your computer will send the BBS machine a request for all
     messages newer than that date, and will then update the message
     base on your machine, and log the last time of update.  If you
     ever want to rescan the full message base on the BBS, just delete
     the S:VBBS.LASTREAD file and call VBBS.

     Now how do you go about reading messages?  Well, you use a new
     stand alone program called "READ".  This is similar in use to the
     old message reading window, but since the data comes directly from
     your disks it will be faster.  Note also that this in not a part
     of TERM and will be updated separately.

     Now you can do all your message reading off line, but what about replys
     and new postings?  Well READ allows you to make new postings and replys
     just as the old message function did (using whatever editor you
     like), and it knows how to maintain the linkages between messages
     for you.  So reading while off line and composing replys and postings
     becomes simplicity itself.

     But how do the new messages get back onto VBBS?  Well every time
     you select the "Messages" gadget in TERM your system not only
     requests an update, but at the same time scans your outgoing
     mail directories (which are within the MESSROOT) and sends VBBS
     any new postings or replys it finds there, and at the same time
     removes them from your system.

44   Beta test release to Ian Gunn.

43   Fixed a bug in the protocol that was causing a crash on exit.

42   Changed the relative priorities of the get files requester and the
     message reader, so that the get files requester still responds
     during a large message download.

     Fixed a number of small bugs so it should now run clean under
     the enforcer and io_torture.  There still seems to be an
     occasional memory overwrite detected when run under MungWall.

41   Added project icon support.  Now TERM can also be started from a
     project icon (rather than just a tool icon).  This allows you to
     have one copy of TERM somewhere on your disks and a bunch of PROJECT
     icon's (each with different settings in their TOOLTYPES) each of
     which uses TERM as its "Default Tool".  By doing this you can log
     on under different names, with different dial up scripts, different
     colours, or even different window snapshots.  Additionally, if there
     is a TERM.info in the same directory as the TERM, then TERM will will
     read its tooltypes array first (before reading the project icon) to
     establish the default settings to be used, it will then read the
     project icon to determine any special settings for this invocation.

     Fixed a bug in the multiplexer where it would get confused and
     reuse a channel that was already in use, resulting in crossed
     connections.

40   Fixed a couple of minor bugs in the get file requester, the
     stray highlighting and the "miss-behaviour" of the sliders.

39   Added highlighting of the file/directory name that is clicked on
     in the get file requester (an UUG request).  Added the ability
     for the uploader of a file to supply the long description at the
     time he uploads the file.  The upload procedure works as before,
     except you will be asked if you want to enter a file description.
     If you answer yes to this TERM will fire up your editor and you can
     enter the file description there, saving and exiting the editor
     as is normally done in the reply/post message function.  TERM
     will take care of the rest.  There is actually no limit to the
     length of file descriptions, but it is recommended to keep them
     to less than a screen full.  Please include the author's name
     and a version number if applicable.  You may include an upload
     credit too.

     IMPORTANT: this version is supposed to fix the "resident Shell-Seg"
     and WorkBench bug.  Test this version with and without the
     resident Shell-Seg line in your startup sequence (running TERM
     from the WorkBench) and let me know if the message reader does
     not crash or lock up after a message posting or reply.

     Some system requesters used to always appear on the workbench
     screen, these should now appear on TERM's custom screen.  Now when
     the "Warning: you have been inactive too long" requester appears
     just clicking on the "Yes" gadget is enough to get the BBS end
     to give you another 7 minutes in which to sleep.

38   Rearranged the colour palette, swapping the black and white entries
     so that they now match with intuitions way of doing things.  If
     you do not adjust the "PALETTE=000" and "PALETTE=FFF" entries
     (swap them) your 3D gadgets will appear "wrong".  When the you
     have been idle timeout message appears the TERM (or WorkBench
     if you run term there) screen should pop to the front.  Fixed
     the bug in window position snapshotting that was preventing the
     message reader and conference windows from staying where you put them.
     Actually this would affect any window you tried to snapshot into
     the lower half of an interlaced screen.  Made the 3D gadgets change
     their rendering to suit the aspect ratio of the screen that is
     being used (interlaced or non-interlaced) to improve their
     appearance on a non-interlaced screen.  If windows are snapshotted
     on a huge screen and TERM is then told to use a smaller screen
     windows which would have appeared outside the small screen will
     be opened so they fit on the smaller screen.

     Added the first cut at message selection requester for the new
     messagebase.  It does not do anything, but shows you what sort of
     controls there will be.  In order to access this window you will
     have to delete any CONTROLWINDOW= tooltypes that may exist in
     your TERM icon.

37   Revised the 3D gadgets slightly (thickened the border slightly).
     Added two additional toggle gadgets to Get Files requester.
     If the Describe gadget is selected (pushed in) you will be
     sent file descriptions as soon as you click on the file name.
     If it is not selected you will not see any descriptions.  If
     the Confirm gadget is selected the system will ask you if you
     want to download the file before allowing the download to start,
     if it is not selected the download will begin as soon as you
     click on the file name.

36   Started revising the interface to include "3D" style gadgets.
     Note, for these to look their best you will have to run TERM
     on a custom screen, it will still run on the WorkBench however.
     Now the horizontal slider in the get files window will not move
     when you hit the resize gadget.  The AutoRequest() style of
     requester have all been changed to match the new 3D type look
     (also this removes one of the buggy CBM routines from use...

35   Just bug fixes.  Fixed it so that the "Idle reminder" now shows
     up on the TERM screen rather than the WorkBench.  The bug in
     parsing the tooltypes that was causing "USER" and "USERWINDOW"
     to be confused is fixed.  Added a requester to the closewindow
     button on the message reader, this will popup if you hit the
     close gadget before a message you recently posted (or replied)
     is still in the queue to be transmitted.  If you reply "yes" to
     this requester (saying you want to quit) there is a risk that part
     of your message will be lost.  On KenW's request I have added an
     additional TOOLTYPE (the TEMPDIR= tooltype) that will let you
     select a directory (other than the default "t:") in which TERM
     will place the message files so that they may be edited.

     Note:  an important bug (which I cannot explain) has been discovered,
     if you find that the message reader locks up or crashes after
     posting or replying to a message -- and you are running TERM
     from the WorkBench -- remove the line in your startup-sequence
     which says "resident Shell-Seg" or else run TERM from the CLI.

34   Added some of the window position and size snapshotting.  Note
     the snapshot menu item appends a tooltype to your TERM.info
     file (icon).  These tooltypes are read from first to last, so if
     there are multiple occurances of a particular window's tooltype
     only the last entry will have any effect -- TERM will not overwrite
     existing tooltypes in case you change your mind later.

33   Added some small fixes for the custom screen stuff -- now your
     editor should open up on the VBBS custom screen when you reply
     or post a message.  There are some new "customize" menus in this
     one, but they didn't do anything...

32   Added even more WorkBench support.  Now the s:vbbs.init file is
     no longer needed.  Instead all the contents of that file are
     stored as TOOLTYPEs entries in the TERM icon.  The following
     new TOOLTYPEs are available: (notes is ()'s explain what eash does)

          USER=(put you user name here)
          PASSWORD=(put your password here)
          EDITOR=(put your editor's name here)
          DOWNDIR=(this is the directory you want downloads to go to)
          KEEPFILE=(this is the file where you want kept messages stored)
          CONFHISTORY=(this is how many lines of conference to buffer)
          HAILSTRING=(this is your conference "hailing" string
                      use it to set up some initials and fancy font
                      or colours for your message text)
          TAILSTRING=(this is your conference "tailing" string, use
                      is to reverse the effects of your HAILSTRING
                      so that other user's messages are not affected)

     Added the ability to run TERM on its own screen.  If you specify
     one of:

          SWIDTH=(640|greater)
          SHEIGHT=(200|greater)
          SDEPTH=(1|2|3|4)

     in the TERM icon's tooltypes array, TERM will open its own custom
     screen and all its windows will appear on it.  This really helps
     to unload the WorkBench, so give it a try!  The SWIDTH specifies
     the width of the screen in pixels, it must be at least 640 wide.
     The SHEIGHT specifies the number of lines on the display, if you
     select 400 you will get an interlaced display.  SDEPTH is the
     number of bit planes to use for the screen, 1 gives you 2 colours,
     2 gives you 4, 3 gives you 8 and 4 gives you 16.  I strongly advise
     against using the 16 colour option, especially if you are running
     on an unexpanded system.

     So that you can set up custom colours for your custom screen the
     TERM program recognizes the PALETTE tool type to specify a custom
     colour.  If you are running a 2 bitplane (4 colour) screen you
     will need 4 lines of PALETTE commands.  For example:

           PALETTE=AAA
           PALETTE=000
           PALETTE=FFF
           PALETTE=679

     will give you a custom screen with the same colours as 2.0 uses.
     Craig Lever suggests a palette of 000, AA9, F70, 008.

31   Fixed bug with scroll bars in file requester where scrolling to
     the bottom of either the file or directory lists and then selecting
     the last item (which was the only one on the display) would cause
     the first item in that list to be selected.

     Added WorkBench support for TERM.  You can now run TERM from the
     workbench just by double clicking its icon.  Note that you must
     set the various default parameters in the icon's TOOLTYPEs array.
     The valid types are:

         BAUD=(300|1200|2400|4800|9600|19200|38400|...)
         DEVICE=(serial.device|modem0.device|siosbx.device|...)
         HANDSHAKE=(none|NONE|7wire)
         UNIT=(0|1|2|3|...)
         SCRIPT=(name of the script file you want run, including directories)

     The various parameters you can choose from are shown in brackets
     separated by vertical bars.

     So to set your TERM to use 2400 baud, no handshaking, using ASDG's
     serial device, unit 1, and to run the demo_script found in your
     ram: directory you would have the following tooltypes array:

         BAUD=2400
         DEVICE=siosbx.device
         HANDSHAKE=none
         UNIT=1
         SCRIPT=ram:demo_script

30   Fixed a few odd bugs in the conference window -- relating to the
     the scrolling and resizing of sliders.  Added file download
     resume capability.  All the user has to do to make use of this
     is to leave partially downloaded files in his download directory
     and then reselect them for download in a later session, the
     TERM program will then see that a partial file exists and will
     ask the user if he wants to resume the download.  Note, to
     guard against corruption of data, the last 1000 bytes of the
     file will be retransmitted.

29   Added extended file descriptions, these appear on demand in
     pop up windows.  If a file description is not available a small
     sysop-defined message will be shown instead.

28   Changed some internal routines to make things a bit more stable.
     Also changed the way that processes are spawned, so force saving
     of all registers (also should help prevent the odd crashes).
     Added the "keep file" function to the message reader.  When you
     hit the "k" key the current message will be appended to your
     private keep file.  Note: this change means an additional line
     has been added to the vbbs.init file, this new line specifies the
     name of the keep file.  See the sample vbbs.init file for the
     location.

27   Added a local deadbeat timer, that will pop open a system requester
     to tell you to do something before the BBS end shuts you down.
     When this pops up protocol activity is suspended until you
     respond to the requester, and then you should do something that
     the BBS end will hear -- opening and then closing the Chat window
     will surfice -- to reset the dead beat timer on the BBS end.

     Also added a pair of string gadgets to the "User Window" in the
     Conference function.  These are to allow you to change your
     prefix/postfix strings on the fly, to ease the pains of editing
     them and testing them.  Note the contents of these gadgets
     are not saved to your S:VBBS.INIT file, so you must make any
     changes to the file yourself.  Also note that this allows you to
     set up several conference windows on your screen and assign
     a different name to each of them, this will allow multiple people
     to share the same Amiga and participate in extended conferences.
     (Try it Sami and Nadeem).

     Added paging of the output that appears in the Sig Reader window.
     Scrolling will automatically pause when the screen fills, and
     when the SPACE BAR is hit the next page will scroll onto the
     screen...

26   Fixed a bug in the "VBBS Message" window which would prevent
     some users from seeing the popup message window, instead they
     would see the "Data but no server" error message.  Also fixed
     a bug in TERM (and in VBBS) which would prevent the system from
     completely hanging up if several things were active at once, this
     would happen quite rarely -- about once in two days at the BBS
     end so most TERM users would never see this one.

     Enhanced the scrolling of the VBBS Message window and the conference
     window.

25   Added a "VBBS Message" window, for the moment this will popup
     whenever you login to the system.  In the future this will be
     used to display other messages of interest to you (such as
     error messages and status information).  Added some more Forbid()
     Permit() pairs around critical code.

24   Added a number of Forbid/Permit pairs to critical places.  Added
     a "Do you want to post this message?" requester which will pop up
     after you have saved a new message.  Note that if your editor
     has the "detach from CLI" feature this requester will appear
     just as the editor is starting up.  If this is the case DO NOT
     ANSWER the requester until AFTER you have saved and exited the
     editor.

     Note this and a few future archives will contain two versions
     of TERM, one called TERM and another called TERM.ae, this second
     version of TERM is for those people who have the Applied Engineering
     internal modems.  While the TERM.ae will work with all external
     modems it is not advised that you do so, since this version does
     all I/O in single character packets, thus slowing things down
     quite a bit.

23   Added a scrollable history buffer to the conference window.  Also
     this is user settable as to the number of messages it keeps, to
     set the maximum number of messages you need to edit your vbbs.init
     file (again).

22   Fixed the bug in the file download requester which could cause
     a system crash if you clicked on gadgets while the initial
     directory tree information was being sent.  Added two new lines
     at the end of the VBBS.INIT file.  These lines contain text or
     control characters you want sent with each paragraph you type in
     the conference window to identify your messages.  The first line
     is the text you want put at the start of your message and the
     second line is the text you want put at the end of your message.

21   Added the multi-chat conference function.  This is a chat window
     like function that allows you to talk to other users who are on
     the system.  You type into the User Window and when you hit the
     return key your message is sent to the BBS, once it gets there
     it is echoed to all users who are on line and in the conference.
     The echoed message will be printed in the conference window.  Note
     that there is no way of identifing who is saying what at the moment,
     this will be added later.

20   Fixed a few bugs related to the scroll bars in the Get File requester.
     Added horizontal scrolling to the Get File requester, and added the
     file date and time to the file requester.  Moved the location of
     the file size.

19   Added sorting to file and directory names in the Get Files requester.
     Added sliders and real parent gadget to Get Files requester.

18   Some minor bug fixes.  Released now because I am just about to
     start on the enhanced message system.

17   Added some more Forbid/Permit pairs around the FindTask calls.
     Revised the launch() function to avoid the bug with starting
     processes when doing downloads.  This resulted in the download
     window appearing briefly and then disappearing...

16   Fixed an odd bug with the download file requester, if you descended
     the directory tree and then went back up using the "parent" gadget
     the file list section would not get updated correctly (the names
     on the screen were fine, but selecting a file would bring up the
     file name of one of the deeper directories.) Added an "about" item to
     the control window's menu.  Fixed two calls to closetimer() which
     were not passing the appropriate parameter -- this could cause
     a GURU.

15   THROW AWAY 14!  V. 15 fixes the bug that was introduced in 14
     that prevented file transfers from working.  This was one of
     those "remove a line of code to fix it" bugs.  This version also
     adds the name of the file being uploaded to the send files window.

14   Some more bugs fixed.  In particular the favourite "I'm getting
     messages going into my chat window" bug has been fixed.
     When multiple windows are opened (for example 2 chat windows)
     TERM will now try not to perfectly cover the first window with
     the second, instead the second window will be opened a bit lower
     down the screen.  This also applies to file transfer and Talk
     windows.  The resetting of the modem at the BBS end has been
     improved, it should be easier to log on now.  The Get Files
     requester has been enhanced: it is now resizable and shows the
     file sizes too.  The "scrolling bug" in the requester may have
     been fixed in the process...  TERM will now tell you if it could
     not open the script file.  And the Send Files window now displays
     the bytes remaining to be sent.

13   Some potential bugs in the Multiplexer and Protocol tasks have
     been fixed.  As well some memory was not being freed by the
     protocol task, this is now freed.  No new features in this
     release.  Added distinct names to the various sub-tasks so that
     it is easier to figure out what was left over from a crash.

12   Default handshaking has now been set to "none", fixed the bug that
     could produce garbaged file names when downloading multiple files
     at once.  Added close window gadgets to the send and receive files
     windows allowing one to abort an in-progress file transfer from
     either end.  Note that doing so will cause a "data received, but
     no server" (or similar) error message to appear in the title bar
     of the control window -- this is normal.

11   Some modifications to the low level protocol.  Added the command
     line switch "-h" to specify the handhaking mode.  You can select
     no handshaking with "-hn" and the RTS/CTS (7-wire mode) with "-h7".

10   Added comments to the script files.  Added the "print" and "char"
     commands.  Removed the echo of all data sent to the serial port.
     Changed the version numbering scheme.

0.9  Added script files, first draft.  Added a server command to close
     a client packet -- now you can close talk and chat windows that
     were opened from the other end of the connection.  Also if the
     other end cannot start the required server (not enough memory...)
     the window that you opened will automatically close down.

0.8  Added the file library window.

0.7  Fixed bug in Talk window, added command line baud rate specification,
     added PACE packects to Send Files.

0.6  Added talk window, revised VBBS.INIT file, fixed editor selection.
     fixed some bugs in low level protocol

0.5  Added message section.

0.4  Initial public testing release.


--------------------------  THANKS ---------------------------

Thanks to all those who suffered through the bugs in various early
versions of VBBS and TERM, your testing is much appreciated.  And
special thanks go to Ian Gunn for the loan of his spare modem!

Another round of thanks go to the AMiga Users of Calgary (AMUC) for
their loan of two modems and phone lines for testing some of the
multi-line facilities.


My thanks also go out to US Robotics for allowing me to purchase a
pair of their Dual Standard V32bis/HST modems on their developer
support program.  Highly recommended modems, these babies really
truck!
